---
import ProsePage from '../../../components/ProsePage.astro';
import { useTranslations, localizedPath } from '../../../i18n/utils';
import { breadcrumbs, graph, HOME_LABEL } from '../../../lib/schema';
import { pdfTools } from '../../../data/pdf-tools';
import { imageTools } from '../../../data/image-tools';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const t = useTranslations(lang);

const browserToolCount = pdfTools.length + imageTools.length;

const description =
  'How OMXHub handles your data: anonymous Google Analytics 4 statistics, AdSense advertising and cookies, affiliate disclosure, and why files opened in our PDF and image tools never leave your device.';

const schema = graph(
  breadcrumbs(lang, [{ name: HOME_LABEL[lang], path: '/' }, { name: t('footer.privacy') }])
);
---
<ProsePage
  lang={lang}
  title="Privacy Policy"
  metaTitle="Privacy Policy — OMXHub"
  description={description}
  crumb={t('footer.privacy')}
  schema={schema}
  updated="2026-08-07"
  updatedLabel="7 August 2026"
  lede="This page explains what is collected while you browse OMXHub, who collects it, and why. It was written for this site specifically and describes what it actually does — not a generic template."
>
  <h2>Introduction</h2>
  <p>
    OMXHub is a content and free-tools site. It does not ask you to create an
    account or sign in. We do not collect your name, your email, or any
    identifying information unless you write to us yourself, and we do not sell
    data to anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things genuinely happen: an analytics service measures how the site is
    used, anonymously, and an ad network serves the advertising that keeps the
    content free. Both are Google services, and both are set out in detail below.
    This policy covers the <strong>omxhub.com</strong> domain in both its Arabic
    and English versions.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      One important note before anything else: the PDF and image tools —
      {browserToolCount} of them — process your files inside your browser, and
      the documents you open in them are not uploaded to OMXHub servers. There is
      a dedicated section on this below.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Usage analytics (Google Analytics 4)</h2>
  <p>
    We use Google Analytics 4 to see which pages are actually read and which find
    no readers, which is what decides what we write and build next. What reaches
    us is aggregated and anonymised: we cannot see who you are, and we cannot tie
    a visit to a specific person.
  </p>
  <p>What is collected in this context typically includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The pages you visited and how long you stayed on them.</li>
    <li>Device type, browser, and operating system.</li>
    <li>Country or city at an approximate level — not a precise location.</li>
    <li>How you arrived: a search engine, an external link, or directly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Google Analytics 4 does not store your IP address as part of the retained
    data; it uses the IP only to estimate approximate location and then discards
    it. You can opt out of measurement entirely with the
    <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Analytics opt-out browser add-on</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Advertising (Google AdSense)</h2>
  <p>
    The site displays advertising through Google AdSense. It is the main way the
    hosting and development costs are covered, which is what keeps the tools free
    with no daily limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    As a third-party vendor, Google uses cookies to serve ads based on your prior
    visits to this site or to other sites on the internet. Google and its
    partners may also use an advertising identifier to personalise what you are
    shown, measure its performance, and avoid repeating the same ad to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not control the content of those ads or the data Google collects
    through them, and we see no personal data by way of them. To control or turn
    off personalised advertising:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://adssettings.google.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Ads Settings</a> — turn off personalisation on your account.</li>
    <li><a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How Google uses cookies in advertising</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">aboutads.info</a> — opt out across several ad networks at once.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    A cookie is a small text file your browser stores so something can be
    remembered between visits. On OMXHub they come from exactly two sources:
  </p>
  <div class="facts">
    <div class="fact">
      <b>Google cookies (third party)</b>
      <span>
        Set by Analytics and AdSense to measure usage and serve advertising. We
        neither own nor read them, and they are governed by Google's policy
        rather than ours.
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="fact">
      <b>One local key of our own</b>
      <span>
        A key named <code>omxhub-theme</code> remembers whether you chose dark or
        light mode. It stays on your device, is never sent to any server, is not
        a cookie, and tracks nothing.
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    You can delete cookies or disable them entirely from your browser settings —
    Chrome, Firefox, Safari and Edge all offer this under privacy settings.
    Disabling them does not stop you using the site: the content and the tools
    work exactly as before, and the advertising simply becomes less relevant to
    you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth knowing: Google's scripts are not loaded with the initial page. They
    are deferred until your first interaction, or until the page goes idle — a
    decision originally made for loading speed, with the side effect that a quick
    visit executes no third-party script at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Affiliate links</h2>
  <p>
    Some links pointing to tool websites are affiliate links, and we may earn a
    commission if you subscribe through them — at no extra cost to you, and with
    no change to the price you pay.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      A commission does not buy a score. The order of tools, their ratings, and
      what we write about their drawbacks are unaffected by whether an affiliate
      link exists, and we publish the weaknesses of tools we have an affiliate
      arrangement with just as plainly. Once you follow such a link, the
      destination site's privacy policy applies, not ours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Contacting us</h2>
  <p>
    There is no contact form on OMXHub and no input fields that submit to a
    server. The <a href={localizedPath(lang, '/contact')}>contact page</a> holds
    a direct email address and nothing else, and that is deliberate: a form means
    an intermediary receives your message, while direct email means it reaches
    only us.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do write to us, we see what you send: your email address, the text of
    your message, and any attachment. We use it to reply and for nothing else. We
    do not add you to a mailing list and do not share your message. Our mail is
    handled by Google, so messages are subject to Google's privacy policy while
    passing through its servers.
  </p>

  <h2>Browser-based PDF and image tools</h2>
  <p>
    This is the most important section in the policy, because it contradicts what
    most people reasonably assume about free online tools.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Every PDF and image tool on OMXHub processes your files locally, inside
      your browser, wherever that is possible.
      <strong>Documents you open in these tools are not sent to OMXHub
      servers</strong>, nor to any third-party service. They are not stored, not
      indexed, and not seen by anyone.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    In practice: when you choose a file, the browser reads it from your disk and
    processes it in your device's memory using capabilities already built into
    it, then produces the new file on your machine for you to download. There is
    no upload step in that sequence, and no upload server exists in this project
    at all. Closing the tab clears everything from memory.
  </p>
  <p>That has practical consequences worth stating plainly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No daily file limit, because there is nothing counting them.</li>
    <li>The tools work offline once the page has loaded, since all processing is local.</li>
    <li>File size is limited by your device's memory, not by a server quota.</li>
    <li>Re-encoding an image strips EXIF metadata including GPS coordinates — helpful when publishing, but worth noting if you need that data.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    And if a tool ever genuinely cannot run in the browser, we will say so on its
    page before you use it, rather than quietly shipping a version that uploads
    your files.
  </p>

  <h2>Data storage</h2>
  <p>
    OMXHub is a static site hosted on Netlify. There is no database behind it, no
    account system, and no user storage. We keep no files, no content logs, and
    no personal records — simply because we have nowhere to put them.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is stored on your device: your dark or light mode preference, and the
    cookies Google sets. What is stored at Google: the anonymised statistics,
    under its own retention periods. Netlify, like any host, keeps temporary
    technical server logs including IP addresses for operational and
    abuse-prevention purposes; we do not access those for analytics.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party services</h2>
  <p>These are all the external parties your visit touches, and there are no others:</p>
  <div class="facts">
    <div class="fact">
      <b>Google Analytics 4</b>
      <span>
        Anonymous usage statistics —
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="fact">
      <b>Google AdSense</b>
      <span>
        Advertising —
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How Google uses data on partner sites</a>.
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="fact">
      <b>Netlify</b>
      <span>Site hosting and content delivery, with technical server logs only.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="fact">
      <b>Ko-fi</b>
      <span>
        Optional support platform. You interact with it only if you press the
        support button yourself, and any payment happens entirely on their side —
        we never see your card details.
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    We use no social-network tracking pixels, no heatmaps, no session recording,
    and no cross-site visitor identification tools.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Since we hold no account for you and no personal data, most of the rights
    granted by data-protection law — including the EU's GDPR and Saudi Arabia's
    Personal Data Protection Law — are exercised here directly, with no
    intermediary:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Access and erasure:</strong> there is nothing of yours for us to produce or delete. If you have written to us, you can ask us to delete that correspondence.</li>
    <li><strong>Objecting to tracking:</strong> through your browser settings, the Analytics opt-out add-on, or Google Ads Settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdrawing consent:</strong> by deleting or disabling cookies at any time.</li>
    <li><strong>Data held by Google:</strong> managed directly from <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/data-and-privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">your Google account</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For any privacy request or question, write to us and we will reply.</p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this page when a new section is added to the site or a service
    we rely on changes. The last-updated date at the top is revised with every
    substantive change, so it is the reference for whether the version you read
    is the one in force.
  </p>
  <p>
    And if a change ever widens what is collected about you rather than narrowing
    it, we will not simply move the date — we will say so explicitly on this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact information</h2>
  <p>
    The party responsible for this site is <strong>Omar Zaberi</strong>, owner and
    editor of OMXHub. For any question about this policy or about your data, get
    in touch at <a href="mailto:omar.zaberi@gmail.com">omar.zaberi@gmail.com</a>
    or via the <a href={localizedPath(lang, '/contact')}>contact page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    To read more about the project and its review methodology, see the
    <a href={localizedPath(lang, '/about')}>about page</a>.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
